## PART A — PPT Script (4 slides)

### Slide 1 — The Challenge

**Say this:**

"Every year, thousands of car owners file safety complaints with NHTSA — the U.S. government's vehicle safety agency — describing things like brakes failing, cars losing power, or steering locking up. Each complaint is filed by a different, unrelated person, in their own words, on their own day.

Somewhere hidden in that pile is often the first sign of a defect that will eventually become a full recall. Today, quality and safety engineers manually search this data with keyword search and periodic review. By the time a human notices the pattern, a formal recall is often already underway — the warning arrives after the fact, not before it.

This affects OEM quality and safety engineering teams triaging fleet-wide complaint volume, compliance reviewers who need a defensible evidence trail, and investigation leads deciding whether something is worth a formal review.

Our question: can we build a system that spots these patterns earlier — and can we prove it works, not just claim it?"

**Anticipated Q&A:**

- Q: "Isn't this just a search engine problem?"
  A: "It looks like one, but the hard part isn't finding complaints — it's proving a rising pattern is real, safety-relevant, and not already covered by a known recall. That needs language understanding plus a rigorous, auditable evidence process, not just search."
- Q: "Why hasn't this been solved already?"
  A: "Simple keyword dashboards exist, but almost nobody tests whether early detection actually works against real history. We specifically built and ran that historical test, which is the unusual part."

---

### Slide 2 — Landscape and Differentiation

**Say this:**

"What exists today is mostly manual keyword search, or generic AI summarizers. The problem with generic AI summarizers is they can hallucinate — invent a trend, a count, or a cause that isn't really there. That's dangerous in a safety context.

RecallZero draws a hard line: AI only ever reads one complaint at a time and turns it into structured facts — what broke, what system, what happened. Every number that combines many complaints — counts, trend, severity, the final risk score — is deterministic code, not the AI. Every score can be recomputed and defended.

We also did something most teams will not have time for: we didn't just claim early detection works, we tested it. We took a real historical recall — the 2022 Ford Mustang Mach-E high-voltage battery recall — hid everything that happened after the recall was announced, and asked whether our system would have alerted before the real announcement, using only information available at the time. Then we froze that exact detector and ran it, completely unchanged, against 20 other real vehicles: 10 with real historical recalls across different problems, and 10 with no recall at all, as a control group."

**Anticipated Q&A:**

- Q: "What stops the AI from just making up a severity score?"
  A: "Architecturally, nothing lets it. Severity, trend, and risk are pure arithmetic on dates, counts, and validated safety keywords found in the complaint text. The AI's output is checked by separate rules before it ever counts toward a score."
- Q: "Why not just use a bigger, better LLM end to end?"
  A: "Then every result would only be as trustworthy as one model call, with no way to reproduce or explain a specific number. Splitting responsibilities — AI for language, code for math — means every alert can be independently checked."
- Q: "What makes your validation different from just measuring accuracy?"
  A: "We pre-registered pass/fail criteria before running the test, so we could not move the goalposts afterward. We also hashed the frozen code and configuration so nobody could quietly tweak something mid-experiment."

---

### Slide 3 — Customer Benefit and ROI

**Say this:**

"Here is what this is worth. On the one real historical case we have fully proven, RecallZero would have raised a qualified alert fifteen days before the actual recall was announced — a real historical replay with the future physically deleted from what the detector could see, not a simulation.

On our 20-vehicle validation set, only about eleven percent of totally clean vehicles — no real recall at all — ever triggered an unconfirmed alert. The system does not cry wolf constantly; when it stays quiet, that is meaningful.

Every alert traces back to real, named NHTSA complaint identifiers. An engineer or auditor can click through and read the actual original complaint behind any score — nothing is an unexplainable AI summary with no receipts.

And practically: instead of reading three thousand raw complaints, an engineer gets a short, ranked list of the handful of patterns that actually deserve attention."

**Anticipated Q&A:**

- Q: "Is 15 days a lot? Recalls can take months to organize."
  A: "Exactly the point — even a two-week head start gives an investigation team more time to gather evidence and start root-cause analysis before public pressure forces a rushed response."
- Q: "You said only one case is 'fully proven' — what about the rest?"
  A: "That's the honest answer, and I'll show it directly on the Validation screen — we ran 20 cases and got a mixed, scientifically informative result, not a clean win."
- Q: "What's the actual dollar ROI?"
  A: "We don't have a validated cost model yet — that needs a real deployment partner's investigation costs. What we can quantify today is lead time, false-alarm rate, and reading-time reduction; the dollar figure needs a real deployment to measure."

