### Slide 4 — Architecture

**Say this:**

"Here's how it fits together, top to bottom. Public NHTSA complaint and recall data comes in. NVIDIA NIM reads each complaint narrative and extracts a structured failure signature — system, failure mode, what happened. NVIDIA embeddings plus deterministic clustering group similar complaints by component and root cause. A fully deterministic risk engine then computes severity, trend, persistence, evidence volume, and whether a visible recall already explains the pattern, combining them into one auditable score. If a recall already covers the pattern, the score is suppressed — the recall cross-reference is actually changing behavior, not just displaying a number next to a complaint count. The result becomes an engineering brief and an alert.

On the right, the NeMo Agent Toolkit agent sits on top — when someone asks a plain-English question, it calls these tools and narrates the already-computed answer. It does not invent anything itself.

Underneath all of it is the Recall Time Machine — our leakage-safe historical validation harness, which is how we produced both the 15-day result and the 20-case cohort test."

**Anticipated Q&A:**

- Q: "Where's the actual 'agent' here — isn't this just a script?"
  A: "The NeMo Agent Toolkit layer is the real agent — give it a plain-English goal, and it decides which of five tools to call and narrates the result. The CLI/API underneath is what the agent calls; a human can also call it directly."
- Q: "Why NVIDIA NIM specifically?"
  A: "It runs identically hosted or fully self-hosted locally on hardware like GB10, which matters for data privacy and avoiding external rate limits — and it's the same ecosystem as the agent toolkit and embeddings, so the stack is coherent end to end."
- Q: "What if NIM is down or rate-limited during the live demo?"
  A: "We have a fully offline synthetic demo mode needing no network or API key — our safety net for exactly this situation."

