### Tab 3 — Validation

**Say this while doing it:**

"This is the honest, harder part of the story, and I think it's the most important tab.

We took this exact frozen detector — not retuned, not touched — and ran it against 20 real vehicles: 10 with genuine historical recalls across different problems (brakes, steering, engines, electrical), and 10 control vehicles with no recall at all.

The headline: our preregistered target, set before running this, was not met. Target-qualified sensitivity was one out of ten — ten percent — against a sixty percent target.

But look at this second number: six out of ten positive vehicles produced at least one real alert before their recall. The detector is noticing something in most cases — the gap is specifically in confidently linking that alert to the correct historical recall, a narrower problem than 'did we notice anything at all.'

On the control side, only about eleven percent of clean vehicles ever triggered an unconfirmed alert — so it isn't alerting on everything either.

Below is the full case-by-case table, and at the bottom, what we actually learned — including two ideas we tested and rejected. We tried smarter AI-based semantic matching; it helped a little but wasn't enough alone. We also tested a theory that real defects show a distinctive long-term recurrence pattern that false alarms don't — the data showed the opposite of what we expected, so we dropped that idea rather than force it in.

That's the point of this screen: we are not hiding an imperfect result. We're showing exactly how we tested it, honestly, and what we learned from being wrong."

**Anticipated Q&A:**

- Q: "Doesn't a failed validation undermine your pitch?"
  A: "I'd argue the opposite — it's evidence the validation is real. If every number looked perfect on the first real test, that would be more suspicious, not less. We're showing a system that can fail honestly and explain exactly where and why."
- Q: "What would it take to fix the sensitivity number?"
  A: "It isn't one bug — it splits into at least three separate causes: some cases have a matching problem, some have the risk score itself scoring too low even with a correct match, and some alerts fire on a real but unrelated issue in the same vehicle. Each needs its own fix, and we deliberately avoided rushing one without more evidence, since our own tests showed an intuitively good idea can turn out to be actively wrong."
- Q: "Is this still useful if sensitivity is only 10%?"
  A: "For the literal challenge — flagging a rising component with no open recall today, and explaining why — yes, that part works today, live, independent of this historical accuracy number. The 10% figure is about a much harder, self-imposed bar: proving after the fact that one specific historical recall would have been caught early. We went further than asked and tested ourselves against a stricter standard than required."

